      gen: [],
      qs: [
        ["You want to heat a structure about 4 cm deep in the thigh. Which ultrasound frequency is most appropriate?",["1 MHz","3 MHz","5 MHz","Frequency does not affect depth"],"Lower frequency is absorbed less in superficial tissue, so 1 MHz reaches deeper structures (about 2-5 cm)."],
        ["Which ultrasound frequency is most appropriate for the superficial lateral epicondyle region?",["3 MHz","1 MHz","0.5 MHz","100 Hz"],"3 MHz is absorbed in superficial tissue (about 1-2 cm), which suits bony, thin areas like the elbow."],
        ["An ultrasound unit is on for 2 ms and off for 8 ms in each cycle. What is the duty cycle?",["20%","25%","80%","40%"],"Duty cycle = on ÷ total period = 2 ÷ (2 + 8) = 0.2 = 20%. 25% would be on ÷ off, which is a common mistake."],
        ["Which parameter choice best produces thermal effects with ultrasound?",["100% (continuous) duty cycle","20% pulsed duty cycle","10% pulsed duty cycle","The lowest possible intensity"],"Continuous ultrasound delivers energy without off periods, allowing tissue temperature to rise. Low duty cycles are used for non-thermal effects."],
        ["Two transducers both have an ERA of 5 cm². One has a BNR of 2:1 and the other 6:1. Which is preferred and why?",["The 2:1 transducer, because its beam is more uniform","The 6:1 transducer, because it delivers higher peak intensity","Either, because BNR does not affect safety","The 6:1 transducer, because it heats faster and more evenly"],"A lower BNR means peak intensity is closer to average, producing fewer hot spots and more comfortable, safer treatment."],
        ["A sound head has an ERA of 5 cm². Which treatment area is most appropriate for a thermal ultrasound treatment?",["About 10-15 cm², a few times the ERA","About 50 cm², ten times the ERA","About 150 cm², the whole anterior thigh","About 2 cm², with the sound head held still"],"The treatment area should be only a few times the ERA (texts cite about 2-4 times). Larger areas spread the energy too thin to heat tissue, and a stationary head creates hot spots."],
        ["Stable cavitation and acoustic streaming are examples of which ultrasound effects?",["Non-thermal (mechanical) effects","Thermal effects alone","Electrical (galvanic) effects","Photochemical effects"],"Cavitation and streaming are mechanical, non-thermal effects. They are emphasized with pulsed, low duty cycle ultrasound."],
        ["A patient who is 14 weeks pregnant has low back pain. Is therapeutic ultrasound over the lumbar region appropriate?",["No; ultrasound over the low back in pregnancy is contraindicated","Yes, if it is delivered pulsed at a 20% duty cycle","Yes, if 3 MHz is used so the depth stays shallow","Yes, as long as she is past the first trimester"],"Ultrasound is contraindicated over the abdomen, pelvis, and low back in pregnancy because of possible effects on the fetus. Changing parameters does not remove the contraindication."],
        ["A patient had a lumbar laminectomy 4 months ago and now has paraspinal muscle tightness at that level. What is the main concern with ultrasound directly over the surgical site?",["Possible exposure of spinal cord (CNS) tissue to ultrasound","Ultrasound will reflect off the hardware and heat the gel","Ultrasound will dissolve scar tissue too quickly","There is no concern because the incision has healed"],"Laminectomy removes bone that normally shields the cord, so ultrasound over the site may reach CNS tissue, which is a contraindication."],
        ["During continuous ultrasound, a patient reports a deep, aching pain under the sound head. What should the clinician do first?",["Stop or lower intensity and check head movement and gel","Increase intensity to push past the patient's discomfort","Hold the sound head still over the most painful point","Shrink the treatment area but keep the same intensity"],"Deep aching suggests periosteal overheating. Reduce intensity, keep the head moving, and ensure adequate coupling."],
        ["Ultrasound is set to 1.5 W/cm² at a 20% duty cycle. What is the temporal average intensity?",["0.3 W/cm²","1.5 W/cm²","7.5 W/cm²","0.2 W/cm²"],"Temporal average = spatial average x duty cycle = 1.5 x 0.2 = 0.3 W/cm². Answering 0.2 confuses the duty cycle itself with intensity."],
        ["Why must stretching be done during or right after thermal ultrasound?",["Tissue temperature falls within minutes after treatment","Ultrasound heating keeps rising for an hour afterward","Stretching blocks the ultrasound from heating tissue","Collagen extensibility only rises after the tissue cools"],"Deep tissue cools quickly once the sound stops, so the stretching window is brief. Waiting lets tissue return toward baseline temperature."],
        ["You need to treat the lateral ankle ligaments, an irregular bony surface. Which technique is MOST appropriate for coupling?",["Water immersion with the head about 1 cm away","Dry contact with no gel to avoid slipping","A thick towel between the head and the skin","A thin coat of lotion with the head kept still"],"Immersion couples ultrasound well over irregular surfaces where gel contact is poor. Air and towels block transmission, and a stationary head creates hot spots."],
        ["Which tissue absorbs ultrasound energy MOST readily?",["Tendon","Subcutaneous fat","Blood","Muscle at rest"],"Tissues high in collagen and protein, such as tendon, ligament, and capsule, absorb ultrasound more than fat or blood. Bone absorbs and reflects even more."],
        ["Which ultrasound effect is harmful and avoided with correct technique?",["Unstable (transient) cavitation","Stable cavitation","Acoustic streaming","Mild tissue heating"],"Unstable cavitation involves violent bubble collapse that can damage tissue. Stable cavitation and streaming are the intended non-thermal effects."]
      ]
